<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Igun Street</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Igun Eronmwon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Igun Street, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igun-eronmwon">Igun Eronmwon</a>, is the quarter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-city">Benin City</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> occupied by the royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a> of bronze casters. For centuries its members made the brass heads, plaques and figures for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-dynasty">Oba</a>'s court using the lost-wax method. The guild survived the British sack of the city in 1897 and its members still cast brass works there today.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Igun Street lies off Sakponba Road in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-city">Benin City</a>, the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-state">Edo State</a>, a short distance from the Ring Road Roundabout. It is the home of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">Guild</a> of Benin Bronze and Brass Casters, the Igun-Eronmwon, and tourists, art dealers and collectors come to watch the whole process by which the castings are made.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The casters' quarter is traced to the second <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ogiso-dynasty">Ogiso</a> period of the first Benin dynasty, under Ogiso Ere, when it was called Obayagbon, meaning all land belongs to the king. It lay within the royal palace grounds until 1897. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esigie">Oba Esigie</a> (1504–1550) casting grew into elaborate forms such as plaques, used for religious, record-keeping and commercial purposes, and Esigie confirmed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a>'s monopoly of the trade and gave it quarters of its own.</p><p>In November 1899 seven royal chiefs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> reported that the quarter had been damaged in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-expedition-of-1897">Benin Expedition of 1897</a>. When the throne was restored to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eweka-ii">Eweka II</a>, the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovonramwen">Oba Ovonramwen</a>, in 1914, some of the craftsmen who had fled returned and the quarters moved to Igun Street. They were encouraged to copy the objects the British had taken, and restrictions on selling their work were lifted. Oba <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akenzua-ii">Akenzua II</a> (1933–1978) continued this work and called for the return of the looted objects.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Only descendants of the original seven families of Igun Street may practise as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> bronze and brass casters, a right traced to Igueghae, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a>'s first leader. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-of-benin">Oba of Benin</a> holds authority over their work and grants franchises to craftsmen with ancestral ties to the street. The guild is headed by the hereditary Inneh N’Igun-Ẹrọnmwọn, with a literate member appointed as secretary. Its techniques are kept from outsiders, and every buyer of a casting receives a receipt, with a duplicate kept by the workshop, as proof of purchase.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Igun Street” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The casters lived in a quarter of their own under the Oba’s direct control; their guild still works in <b>Igun Street</b>.</blockquote><a href="/societies/benin">Social Hierarchy of the Kingdom of Benin</a></li><li><blockquote>Eweka II rebuilt the royal palace and revived court ceremonies and the royal guilds, including the bronze casters of <b>Igun Street</b>, who continue to make brass works.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-benin-restored">Kingdom of Benin (restored)</a></li></ul></section>
